In LaTeX, the `\rowcolor` command is used to set the background color of a row in a table. It is typically used within the `colortbl` package, which extends the capabilities of the `array` and `tabular` environments to include colored cells and rows.

### Basic Usage:
```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[table]{xcolor} % Loads colortbl automatically

\begin{document}
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\rowcolor{lightgray} % Sets the background color of the current row
Cell 1 & Cell 2 \\
\hline
Cell 3 & Cell 4 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{document}
```

### Key Points:
1. **Package Requirement**: You need to load the `colortbl` package (or `xcolor` with the `table` option, as shown above).
2. **Placement**: `\rowcolor` must be placed at the beginning of the row (before any cell content).
3. **Color Options**: You can use predefined colors (e.g., `lightgray`, `yellow`, `red!30` for 30% red) or define custom colors using `\definecolor`.

### Example with Custom Color:
```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[table]{xcolor}
\definecolor{mycolor}{RGB}{200, 230, 255} % Define a custom color

\begin{document}
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\rowcolor{mycolor}
Cell 1 & Cell 2 \\
\hline
Cell 3 & Cell 4 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{document}
```

### Notes:
- If you use `\rowcolor` in a row with `\hline`, the color may not extend under the `\hline`. To fix this, use `\arrayrulecolor` to set the line color to match the background.
- For alternating row colors, you can manually apply `\rowcolor` to each row or use packages like `xcolor` with `\rowcolors` for automation.

### Alternating Row Colors:
```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[table]{xcolor}
\rowcolors{2}{gray!20}{white} % Alternates between gray and white starting from row 2

\begin{document}
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
Header 1 & Header 2 \\
\hline
Cell 1 & Cell 2 \\
Cell 3 & Cell 4 \\
Cell 5 & Cell 6 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{document}
```

This will color the rows alternately starting from the second row. Adjust the starting row and colors as needed.

